NNAAMMEE
       null, zero - data sink

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       Data written to the _/_d_e_v_/_n_u_l_l and _/_d_e_v_/_z_e_r_o special files is discarded.

       Reads  from  _/_d_e_v_/_n_u_l_l always return end of file (i.e., rreeaadd(2) returns
       0), whereas reads from _/_d_e_v_/_z_e_r_o always return  bytes  containing  zero
       ('\0' characters).

       These devices are typically created by:

           mknod -m 666 /dev/null c 1 3
           mknod -m 666 /dev/zero c 1 5
           chown root:root /dev/null /dev/zero

FFIILLEESS
       _/_d_e_v_/_n_u_l_l
       _/_d_e_v_/_z_e_r_o

NNOOTTEESS
       If these devices are not writable and readable for all users, many pro‐
       grams will act strangely.

       Since  Linux 2.6.31, reads from _/_d_e_v_/_z_e_r_o are interruptible by signals.
       (This change was made to help with bad latencies for large  reads  from
       _/_d_e_v_/_z_e_r_o.)

